---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/marketing.css';
import {getComparison} from '../../data/comparisons';

const c = getComparison('openhands')!;

const title = 'OpenHands Alternative: Self-Hosted Agent Platform | GratefulAgents';
const description =
  'GratefulAgents vs OpenHands: both open-source self-hosted agent harnesses compared on Kubernetes depth, client apps, trigger integrations, and observability.';

const faqs = c.faqs;

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/openhands/#article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/openhands/',
    author: {'@type': 'Organization', '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
    publisher: {'@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/openhands/#breadcrumb',
    itemListElement: [
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Home',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Alternatives',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 3,
        name: 'OpenHands',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/openhands/',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/alternatives/openhands/#faq',
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.question,
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: f.answer,
      },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Base {title} {description} type="article" {structuredData}>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="mk-page">

      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol class="mk-breadcrumb">
          <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
          <li><a href="/alternatives/">Alternatives</a></li>
          <li><span aria-current="page">OpenHands</span></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="mk-layout-2col">
        <div>

          <div class="mk-hero">
            <div class="mk-vs-badge">
              <span class="mk-vs-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              Comparison
            </div>
            <h1>GratefulAgents vs OpenHands</h1>
            <p>
              OpenHands (formerly OpenDevin) is a well-established, open-source coding agent
              framework with a large community and strong research backing. GratefulAgents is also
              open source and self-hosted. The meaningful comparison here is not open vs closed—
              both are genuinely open source. It is about scope: a container-first agent harness
              versus a Kubernetes-native control plane built around that harness.
            </p>
          </div>

          <article class="mk-prose">

            <h2>What OpenHands is</h2>
            <p>
              OpenHands (previously called OpenDevin) is an open-source autonomous coding agent
              framework developed by All Hands AI and a large contributor community. It started as
              a research project exploring how LLMs could perform software engineering tasks
              autonomously, and has grown into a mature, actively maintained platform with serious
              benchmark results and widespread adoption.
            </p>
            <p>
              The architecture is container-first: OpenHands runs a runtime Docker container that
              gives the agent a sandboxed environment with a shell, browser, and editor. You
              configure it with your model provider (it supports a wide range of models via
              LiteLLM), point it at your codebase, and give it tasks. The web UI provides
              interaction, session replay, and history.
            </p>
            <p>
              OpenHands is one of the most accessible self-hosted agent options: a Docker Compose
              command is sufficient to get started on any machine with Docker installed, without
              Kubernetes.
            </p>

            <h2>What OpenHands is genuinely good at</h2>
            <p>
              {c.whatItIsGoodAt}
            </p>
            <p>
              If your primary requirement is a proven open-source agent harness that you can run
              today on any Docker-capable machine, OpenHands is a strong, well-validated choice
              that should be evaluated on its own merits. The community, research backing, and
              ease of setup are real advantages.
            </p>

            <h2>How the comparison actually works: scope, not open vs closed</h2>
            <p>
              This comparison is different from the others on this site (GitHub Copilot, Devin),
              which contrast a self-hosted product with cloud SaaS services. OpenHands and
              GratefulAgents are both open source and both self-hosted. Framing this as
              "open vs closed" would be inaccurate and unfair to OpenHands.
            </p>
            <p>
              The real distinction is <em>product scope</em>. OpenHands is a powerful, focused
              agent harness—it solves the core problem of running an agent in an isolated
              environment against a codebase. GratefulAgents ships an entire product built around
              that same core problem, with additional layers for organisations that need them:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <strong>Kubernetes-native control plane with CRDs.</strong> GratefulAgents
                introduces declarative resource types (<code>AgentRun</code>,
                <code>Project</code>, <code>GitHubRepository</code>, <code>LinearProject</code>,
                <code>SlackAgent</code>, <code>Cron</code>) that let you manage agents as
                Kubernetes resources. This enables GitOps-style agent management, RBAC via
                Kubernetes native permissions, and integration with existing cluster tooling.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Multi-platform client applications.</strong> GratefulAgents ships a
                web dashboard, a desktop client for macOS Apple Silicon and Linux (AMD64/ARM64),
                and iOS and Android mobile apps. This matters for teams that need to review,
                steer, or stop agent runs from multiple devices and contexts.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>First-class trigger integrations.</strong> GitHub issues, PR comments, and
                review feedback trigger runs via a <code>GitHubRepository</code> CRD. Linear
                tickets trigger via <code>LinearProject</code>. Slack messages via
                <code>SlackAgent</code>. Scheduled runs via <code>Cron</code>. All declared as
                Kubernetes resources.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Sharing and permissions.</strong> GratefulAgents has a built-in sharing
                and permissions model for controlling who can see and interact with runs and
                projects. OpenHands does not have an equivalent built-in access control layer.
              </li>
              <li>
                <strong>Agent Ops observability console.</strong> Per-run Jaeger traces, cost,
                token counts, tool calls, subagent dependency graphs, compaction events, and
                model comparison. This is a first-class product surface rather than an add-on.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Conversely, OpenHands has real advantages that GratefulAgents does not match:
              a larger and more active community, a longer track record, stronger agent benchmark
              results, and a dramatically lower barrier to entry (Docker Compose vs Kubernetes).
            </p>

            <h2>Dimension-by-dimension comparison</h2>
            <p>
              The following table covers the dimensions where the two projects most meaningfully
              differ. Where a dimension is similar or not clearly distinguishable, it is omitted.
            </p>

            <div class="mk-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Comparison table: GratefulAgents vs OpenHands">
              <table class="mk-compare-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Dimension</th>
                    <th scope="col">OpenHands</th>
                    <th scope="col">GratefulAgents</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {c.rows.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{row.dimension}</td>
                      <td>{row.competitor}</td>
                      <td>{row.gratefulagents}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <h2>Who should pick which</h2>

            <h3>Choose OpenHands if:</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>You want to self-host a capable open-source agent without Kubernetes expertise or infrastructure.</li>
              <li>Docker Compose is your preferred deployment model.</li>
              <li>You value community size, research track record, and benchmark validation.</li>
              <li>You want access to the broadest possible set of LiteLLM-compatible models.</li>
              <li>You need a mature, well-tested framework that has been used in production by many organisations.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Choose GratefulAgents if:</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Your organisation already runs Kubernetes and you want agents managed as native cluster resources via CRDs.</li>
              <li>You need first-class, CRD-backed trigger integrations with GitHub, Linear, Slack, and cron—declared as Kubernetes manifests.</li>
              <li>You need a multi-platform experience: web dashboard, desktop client (macOS/Linux), and mobile apps (iOS/Android) for monitoring and steering runs.</li>
              <li>You need a built-in sharing and permissions model for team access control without building one yourself.</li>
              <li>You want an Agent Ops console with Jaeger-based distributed tracing, per-run cost, and subagent dependency graphs as a first-class feature.</li>
              <li>You are adopting a GitOps approach to infrastructure and want agent configuration version-controlled as Kubernetes manifests.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="mk-callout mk-callout-neutral">
              <strong>Note on current status:</strong> GratefulAgents is in early development.
              OpenHands is more mature with a larger community and more production miles on it.
              If reliability and community support are high priorities, OpenHands has a meaningful
              head start. GratefulAgents is the right bet if the Kubernetes-native product scope
              is what you need and you are willing to adopt early-stage software.
            </div>

            <h2>A note on sandboxing</h2>
            <p>
              Both OpenHands and GratefulAgents run agents in sandboxed environments. OpenHands
              uses a Docker container runtime. GratefulAgents uses
              <a href="https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox" rel="noopener noreferrer">kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox</a>,
              a Kubernetes SIG project providing isolated execution environments at the pod level.
              The Kubernetes-native sandboxing integrates with your cluster's existing network
              policies, RBAC configuration, and pod security standards—which can simplify
              compliance posture for organisations that already have Kubernetes security controls
              in place.
            </p>

            <h2>Running them side by side</h2>
            <p>
              Because both products are self-hosted and open source, there is nothing stopping you
              from running them in parallel during an evaluation. You can direct identical workloads
              at both, compare the results, and make an evidence-based decision. This is the
              recommended approach if you have time—the architectural differences are significant
              enough that a hands-on evaluation will reveal more than any comparison document can.
            </p>

            <h2>How to try GratefulAgents</h2>
            <p>
              The recommended evaluation path is a local Kind cluster. Docker is the only
              prerequisite—no cloud account needed. The
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/">Kind getting-started guide</a>
              walks through bootstrapping the cluster, deploying the Helm chart (including
              PostgreSQL/pgvector, MinIO, and Jaeger), adding your model credentials, and
              triggering a first run. The process takes roughly fifteen minutes.
            </p>
            <p>
              For a more persistent installation on a server, the
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/">k3s guide</a> covers a fresh
              Debian or Ubuntu deployment.
            </p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/" class="btn btn-primary">
                Get started with Kind →
              </a>
              <a href="/alternatives/" class="btn btn-quiet">More comparisons</a>
            </div>

            <hr class="mk-divider" />

            <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
            <div class="mk-faq">
              {faqs.map((f) => (
                <details class="mk-faq-item">
                  <summary>{f.question}</summary>
                  <div class="mk-faq-answer">{f.answer}</div>
                </details>
              ))}
            </div>

          </article>

          <div class="mk-disclaimer">
            <p>
              OpenHands™ is a project of All Hands AI. All trademarks are the property of their
              respective owners. This comparison reflects the authors' understanding of each project
              at time of writing; both projects move quickly and capabilities change frequently.
              Consult
              <a href="https://www.all-hands.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer">All Hands AI's own OpenHands documentation</a>
              for authoritative and up-to-date information.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <aside>
          <div class="mk-sidebar-card">
            <h3>Try GratefulAgents</h3>
            <p>Self-hosted. Open source (AGPL-3.0). Runs in your Kubernetes cluster.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/" class="btn btn-primary">
                Kind quick-start →
              </a>
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/" class="btn btn-quiet">
                k3s on a server
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mk-sidebar-card" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <h3>More comparisons</h3>
            <p>See how GratefulAgents compares to cloud-managed coding agents.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/alternatives/github-copilot/" class="btn btn-quiet">vs Copilot →</a>
              <a href="/alternatives/devin/" class="btn btn-quiet">vs Devin →</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mk-sidebar-card" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <h3>Why self-host?</h3>
            <p>Architecture guide covering data residency, IP protection, and cost control.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/self-hosted-coding-agents/" class="btn btn-quiet">Read guide →</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
